WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.997
NIST PQC standards provide a foundation for migrating away from public-key cryptography that is vulnerable to future quantum attack.

2
00:00:08.997 --> 00:00:18.547
The standards give organizations shared terminology and concrete algorithm references, but they do not perform the migration by themselves.

3
00:00:18.547 --> 00:00:25.729
Security teams still need inventory, planning, testing, vendor engagement, and operational discipline.

4
00:00:25.729 --> 00:00:28.807
FIPS 203 covers ML-KEM for key establishment.

5
00:00:28.807 --> 00:00:31.964
FIPS 204 covers ML-DSA for digital signatures.

6
00:00:31.964 --> 00:00:36.699
FIPS 205 covers SLH-DSA for stateless hash-based digital signatures.

7
00:00:36.699 --> 00:00:41.277
Each standard addresses a different role in cryptographic systems.

8
00:00:41.277 --> 00:00:45.539
Key establishment protects how parties create shared secrets.

9
00:00:45.539 --> 00:00:50.984
Digital signatures protect authenticity, integrity, and trust relationships.

10
00:00:50.984 --> 00:00:55.404
The operational migration questions are different for each role.

11
00:00:55.404 --> 00:01:02.428
Additional algorithms such as Falcon and HQC matter for diversity, backup options, and future planning.

12
00:01:02.428 --> 00:01:06.927
They remind us that PQC migration is not a one-time naming exercise.

13
00:01:06.927 --> 00:01:17.424
The standards ecosystem will continue maturing through additional documents, protocol integration, validation, product support, and field experience.

14
00:01:17.424 --> 00:01:22.475
Organizations should avoid locking themselves into brittle assumptions.

15
00:01:22.475 --> 00:01:25.553
The goal is not merely to know algorithm names.

16
00:01:25.553 --> 00:01:39.759
The goal is to connect standards to real systems: where cryptography exists, what data or trust depends on it, which vendors control it, which protocols carry it, how it will be tested, and how it can be changed later.

17
00:01:39.759 --> 00:01:45.994
PQC readiness is the combination of standards awareness and practical migration execution.
